Why this directory is GPL-3.0-or-later
--------------------------------------
mito_blast_filter re-implements the BLAST-output filtering step used to decide which
assembly contigs are mitochondrial. It was written after reading, and it deliberately
adopts the parameter choices of, two upstream implementations:

  * MitoHiFi, src/parse_blast.py
    Copyright 2020 Marcela Uliano-Silva
    GPL-3.0-or-later (per the file's own header)
    https://github.com/marcelauliano/MitoHiFi

  * Human Pangenome Project, hpp_production_workflows,
    QC/docker/parseMitoBlast/scripts/parse_mito_blast_results/parse_mito_blast_results.py
    a re-tuning of the above, which retains the GPL-3.0-or-later notice
    https://github.com/human-pangenomics/hpp_production_workflows

No code, identifier, comment or structure was copied from either: this is a bash and
awk streaming implementation, whereas both upstreams are pandas dataframe code, and
the central quantity it computes is different (see below). Thresholds, formulas and
command-line flags are in any case not copyrightable subject matter.

The reason for the GPL notice is therefore not that copying occurred, but that the
implementation is not clean-room -- both upstream sources were read, and the specific
combination of the three filters below is an upstream design choice rather than an
independent one. Rather than rely on that combination being unprotectable, this
directory is kept as a self-contained, separately licensed component invoked as an
external tool, so the workflow that calls it stays unambiguously MIT. Note that
MitoHiFi's own repository-level LICENSE is MIT while three of its source files carry
GPL-3.0-or-later headers; that contradiction is unresolved upstream, and honouring the
per-file GPL notice is the conservative reading. HPP made the same choice.

What was adopted
----------------
From HPP (which in turn re-tuned MitoHiFi's 5 / 80 / 50):

  --max-subject-multiple  10.0
  --min-contig-perc       80.0
  --min-coverage-perc     70.0

and, in the calling workflow, `blastn -dust no -soft_masking false` together with the
`-outfmt "6 std qlen slen"` column set.

What differs
------------
Coverage is computed by merging overlapping query intervals, whereas both upstreams sum
each HSP's share of the contig independently. The naive sum double-counts overlapping
HSPs and can exceed 100% -- the worked example recorded in HPP's own pullContigIDs task
reads 100.06% -- which makes the threshold quietly more permissive than it appears. A
contig hit three times over the same 40% of its length scores 120% under the naive sum
and would be deleted at any threshold below that; here it scores 40% and is kept. See
tests/unit_merge.blast.tsv for that case.

The three thresholds are also exposed as options rather than hard-coded, so the values
above are a default chosen for comparability with HPP, not part of the definition of the
method.
